{"id": "197709", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197709", "title": "Tlayacapan", "text": "Tlayacapan is a town in the Central Mexico state of Morelos. The town is one of the most scenic and culturally rich in the region and is designated as one of Mexico's Pueblos Magicos. It is known for its 16th century monastery and many chapels, and is included as part of the \"16th century monasteries on the slopes of Popocatapetl\", a UNESCO World Heritage Site. It is an easy weekend getaway for people in the Mexico City area (though weekend crowds are significantly less than towns closer to the capital).\nUnderstand.\nTlayacapan is a small town with a population of about 7,000.\nHistory.\nLong before the arrival of Spaniards in Mexico, Tlayacapan was an Olmec settlement that was part of the Aztec trading network, a stop over on the way to more southern settlements. \nEarthquakes.\nEarthquakes are a common phenomenon in Central Mexico, which is geologically active. On September 19, 2017, a 7.2 magnitude earthquake shook Tlayacapan, damaging many buildings, including the historic Monastery of San Juan Bautista, and the Palacio Municipal (the oldest city hall in Mexico). The historic church remains closed to the public until restorations can be completed and public safety ensured. Damage to the church includes a fallen ceiling and a major crack that splits the building's facade. \nGet in.\nFrom Mexico City's Taxque\u00f1a bus terminal (south), TER buses (Transportes Estrella Roja) leave hourly for the 2-1/2 hour trip to Tlayacapan and cost M$100 (February 2023). \nFrom Cuernavaca, TER buses leave every 30-40 minutes for the 2 hour trip to Tlayacapan. Tickets cost M$80 (February 2023). \nGet around.\nTlayacapan is a compact small town. Walking is the best way to get around. Taxis are readily available if you want to go further afield.\nBuy.\nA variety of local artesania shops can be found on Calle Emilio Carranza and in the Plaza del Alfarero where a craft market is set up on Saturdays and Sundays."}
{"id": "197732", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197732", "title": "Maze National Park", "text": "Maze National Park is in Gamo-Gofa Zone of Southern Ethiopia.\nUnderstand.\nMaze is named after the largest river that crosses through this park.\nDomba, Zage and Lemase rivers are the main rivers that flow into Maze river.\nMaze national park is one of the last remaining sites and also the second most important site for the conservation of the Swayne Hartebeest after Senkelle Hartebeest Sanctuary. \nHistory.\nMaze National Park was established in 2005 in order to control the hunting that has been happening in the area for many years. This controlled hunting area was developed into a national park because of the presence of Swayne Hartebeest which is native to Ethiopia.\nLandscape.\nIt covers an area of 220 km\u00b2 which include plain, sloppy areas, hills, escarpments and interconnection of mountains at its boundaries.\nFlora and fauna.\nMaze offers all botanists a field trip because it comprises different types of vegetation. The growth of these vegetations seen in different parts of the park is due to different altitudes at different points in the park.\nIt is one of the few parks in Ethiopia that acts as habitat for most of the endangered animals. Animals found in here include the Vervet monkeys, pigs, buffaloes, waterbucks, Orbi, the Swayne\u2019s hartebeest, the Anubus baboon, leopards, the Bohor red buck, warthogs, the lesser Kudu, wild cats, lions. Over 196 bird species can be seen.\nGet in.\nThe park is about 460 km south west of Addis Ababa, 22 km from Selam Ber, and 235 km from Awasa.\nStay safe.\nbecause the accommodation facilities close down, and the park is sometimes not accessible during this time."}
{"id": "197733", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197733", "title": "Howell", "text": "Howell is a township of 54,000 people (2021) in Monmouth County in New Jersey.\nGet in.\nHowell is at the intersection of I-195 (exit 28) and US-9.\nNJ Transit provides bus transportation from:\nAcademy Bus Line's route 9 provides service from the Financial District in Lower Manhattan.\nGet around.\nHowell is mostly suburban with no walkable downtown, so a car is highly recommended."}
{"id": "197742", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197742", "title": "Nech Sar National Park", "text": "Nech Sar National Park, or Nechisar National Park (Amharic: \u12e8\u1290\u127a\u1233\u122d \u1265\u1214\u122b\u12ca \u1353\u122d\u12ad) is in the western part of Southern Ethiopia.\nUnderstand.\nNech Sar got its name from the combination of two Amharic words which means \"white grass\" referring to the central grassy plains associated with Burchell's zebra. This park is between lakes namely Abaya and Chamo. The cliff that separated these two lakes is referred to as \"bridge of God\".\nThe national park covers an area of \u200b\u200b514 km\u00b2.\nLandscape.\nThere is a large steppe grass plain in the center of the park, with light jungle at the edge.\nFlora and fauna.\nThere are 104 species of mammals and 351 species of birds in the park, as well as around 1,000 species of plants.\nMammals native to this park include Swayne\u2019s hartebeests, Scott\u2019s hairy bats, Crocidura phaeura, white-footed rats, Ethiopian grass rats and Hinde\u2019s bat. Other animals include Burchell\u2019s Zebras, Greater Kudu, Grant\u2019s gazelle, Hippopotamuses, crocodiles, Aubis Baboon, black-backed jackals, side-striped jackals, bat-eared foxes, warthogs, African hunting dogs, Guenther\u2019s dikdiks, lions and Leopards.\nClimate.\nThe temperatures are between 17 C and 30 C. The highest temperatures are in January to March, the lowest in November. There are rainy seasons twice a year, from March to May and from September to November. But the amount of precipitation is only 900 mm.\nGet in.\nNechisar is about 2 km from Arba Minch, about 510 km south of Addis Ababa. In the north of Arba Minch you drive past the airport and reach the entrance to the national park.\nFees and permits.\nAdmission for 24 hr: 100 birr per person, fee for own car 100 birr (as of 2016). \nGet around.\nThere are about 180 km of slopes in the park. A four-wheel drive vehicle or a very powerful two-wheel drive vehicle (e.g. Toyota Landcruiser) is required to visit the park. There are very steep slopes to overcome.\nWhen it rains, the ground softens very quickly, so the park cannot be visited.\nIt is not recommended to visit the crocodile market on your own through the jungle, as there are mostly no paths and you are exposed to a high risk.\nSee.\nThere are many Anubis Baboons to be seen in the park, behaving peacefully and slightly shyly. With a little patience, however, you can get quite close to the animals. Otherwise you see a lot of warthogs. On the plain there are herds of Grant's gazelle, Hartebeest and Burchell's zebra. There are also a few lions in Nechisar National Park, but you rarely see them. Other animal species include the greater kudu, lesser kudu, bushbuck, waterbuck, dik-dik, and spotted hyena. In addition to the mentioned baboons, there are also green morecats and mantled monkeys.\nThe Chamosee is teeming with crocodiles and herds of hippos, as well as large birds (pelicans, marabou, ospreys, storks, cormorants).\nDo.\nThe is a stretch of beach on Lake Chamo that is particularly popular with crocodiles. It is possible to book a trip with a motor boat there (1200 birr in 2018). Captains can be arranged free of charge at the tourist office. Besides crocodiles, there are many hippos and large birds (pelicans, marabou, osprey) to see. The boat trip takes about 2 hours. When the water level is high, hippos may not be visible.\nUnder no circumstances should you let any random \"guide\" (not even from the \"Bekele Mola Hotel\"!) mediate you, unless you are willing to pay around 100 birr more.\nThe best time is in the afternoon, when the crocodiles take their digestive sleep in the sun on the shore.\nYou can organise the boat safari with the local guide association or by yourself upon arrival in the park. They will take you over Lake Chamo where you can see crocodiles (at a place called crocodile market) and various birds (2018). On the lake Abayo you reach the shore and a ferry station right behind the crocodile farm and you will walk around with a ranger and you could watch Monkeys, Hornbill, Zebra, Dikdik and other Antelope. 500 birr per person in total (2016) or just a tip for the ferry captain who shows you the way. \nForty Springs is on the road 3 km inside from the entrance and can be reached by foot. The springs are unspectacular. \nBuy, eat, and drink.\nBring everything you need with you.\nSleep.\nHotel accommodation is not available in the national park, but is available in the nearby town of Arba Minch.\nCamping.\nCampsites are designated in the national park, e.g.. The camping fee is 40-60 birr per tent, depending on the tent size (as of 2016)."}
{"id": "197743", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197743", "title": "Kafta Sheraro National Park", "text": "Kafta Sheraro National Park is in Tigray in Northern Ethiopia. It occupies a dry lowland landscape around the Tekeze River and is one of Ethiopia's important protected areas for African elephants. \nUnderstand.\nKafta-Sheraro was recognized as a park in 2007. It is close to the Eritrea border. The protected area is listed as a designated national park under the management authority of the Ethiopian Wildlife Conservation Authority (EWCA).\nKafta-Sheraro is markedly different from the highland landscapes around Mekele. Much of the park consists of dry woodland, savanna, shrubland and riverine vegetation. The Tekeze River and seasonal watercourses provide important habitat in an otherwise dry environment.\nThe park was established as the Shire Wildlife Reserve in the 1960s. It was subsequently proposed for expansion and upgrading, and its national-park status was legally established in 2014. The boundaries and effective area of the park have been affected by subsequent land-use changes; recent research estimates the managed area at about 2,176 km\u00b2, while the World Database on Protected Areas reports a designated area of 1,878 km\u00b2.\nWildlife.\nKafta-Sheraro is particularly important for the conservation of African elephants. Historical surveys recorded a transboundary elephant population shared with Eritrea, with an informed estimate of about 300 elephants in the park in 2014.\nA 2025 study documented 28 species of medium and large mammals in the park, including six globally threatened species. The research also found that wildlife use different habitats according to season and local environmental conditions.\nThe park also supports a variety of birds, reptiles and other wildlife associated with its woodland, grassland and riverine habitats.\nConservation.\nConservation in Kafta-Sheraro faces substantial pressure from surrounding human activities. Agricultural expansion, livestock grazing, settlement, uncontrolled fires, firewood collection, charcoal production and illegal gold mining have all been identified as threats to the park's habitats.\nHuman-elephant conflict is another important issue. Elephants can move into agricultural areas and damage crops, creating tension between wildlife conservation and the livelihoods of communities living around the park. Research published in 2025 found that crop raiding by elephants has increased over the past decade.\nThe Tigray conflict also severely disrupted park management and enforcement. A 2025 assessment reported major losses in conservation capacity during the war and continuing difficulties in restoring effective management because of instability and access problems.\nLandscape.\nThe park's landscape is dominated by dry woodland and savanna, with greener vegetation occurring along rivers and other areas with greater water availability.\nThe Tekeze River is particularly important to the ecology of the region. Riverine areas provide water and vegetation for wildlife and create ecological corridors through the drier landscape.\nFlora and fauna.\nHome to large mammals such as Caracal, Aardvark, Roan antelope, Loxodonta africana, Xerus rutilus, Hystrix cristata, Tragelaphus strepsiceros, Hippotragus equines (Roan antelope), Taurotragus oreyx, rufifrons Thomson\u2019s, Kobus ellipsiprymnus (Defassa waterbuck), Bush duiker, Dikdik, oribi, Oreotragus oreotragus, Common warthog, Ethiopian rock hyrax, White tailed mongoose, Galerella sanguinea (Slender mongoose), Civettictis civetta (African civet), Crocula crocuta (Spotted hyena), Canis aureus (Common jackal), Canis mesomelas (Black-backed jackal), mellivora capensis (Honey badger), Papio anubis.\nClimate.\nKafta-Sheraro National Park has a hot to warm semi-arid climate, typical of the lowland areas of western and northwestern Tigray. The park experiences a pronounced wet season followed by a much longer dry period. Annual rainfall is generally about 400\u2013650 mm, although the amount varies across the park. \nRainfall is concentrated mainly between late May and early September, with the heaviest rainfall generally occurring in July and August.\nGet in.\nLocated 600 km northwest of Mekelle; the capital of Tigray."}
{"id": "197745", "revid": "2302739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197745", "title": "Geraille National Park", "text": "Geraille National Park (also Gerale National Park) is in Liben zone of Ethiopia.\nUnderstand.\nGeraille National Park covers a previously known hunting area in Southern Ethiopia called Borena Controlled Hunting Area. \nHistory.\nEstablished in 2006, the Somali National Regional proposed the establishment in order to moderate different savannah wildlife life including rare animals like, Giraffe, African Elephant and Black Rhinoceros.\nGet in.\nLocated around 900 km southeast of Addis Ababa and 120 km north east of Moyle."}
{"id": "197746", "revid": "2380603", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=197746", "title": "Susu phrasebook", "text": "Susu, also known as Soussou, is a language of the Susu people that reside in Sierra Leone. The dialect is one of the major languages of the people that are found in the coastal region of Guinea."}
